1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,712
Paris.

2
00:00:00,912 --> 00:00:03,428
December the second, eighteen oh four.

3
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:05,463
Notre Dame Cathedral.

4
00:00:05,663 --> 00:00:08,088
Thousands of guests are holding their breath.

5
00:00:08,288 --> 00:00:10,871
That golden crown, flying through the air?

6
00:00:11,071 --> 00:00:13,774
It's supposed to be sitting on a velvet cushion.

7
00:00:13,974 --> 00:00:15,221
In my hands.

8
00:00:15,421 --> 00:00:17,982
And the angry little man about to catch it?

9
00:00:18,182 --> 00:00:20,299
That's Napoleon Bonaparte.

10
00:00:20,499 --> 00:00:24,445
This is the story of how I accidentally ruined his coronation.

11
00:00:24,745 --> 00:00:25,937
Let me back up.

12
00:00:30,337 --> 00:00:35,831
It started at a Napoleon exhibition, in front of a giant copy of a famous painting.

13
00:00:36,031 --> 00:00:39,332
The Coronation of Napoleon, by Jacques-Louis David.

14
00:00:39,532 --> 00:00:42,234
The real one is almost ten metres wide.

15
00:00:42,434 --> 00:00:45,947
And next to it, in a tiny glass case, sat one golden leaf.

16
00:00:46,147 --> 00:00:49,204
The sign said: a leaf from Napoleon's crown.

17
00:00:49,404 --> 00:00:50,512
Do not touch.

18
00:00:51,012 --> 00:00:52,816
You know where this is going.

19
00:00:53,316 --> 00:00:54,554
Spinning clocks.

20
00:00:54,754 --> 00:00:56,301
A flash of gold.

21
00:00:56,501 --> 00:00:59,835
And I fell two hundred and twenty two years into the past.

22
00:01:00,335 --> 00:01:04,510
I landed in a freezing Paris street, in the middle of a huge crowd.

23
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:05,646
Soldiers.

24
00:01:05,846 --> 00:01:06,695
Horses.

25
00:01:06,895 --> 00:01:07,635
Flags.

26
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:10,412
Everyone was shouting: Vive l'Empereur!

27
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:12,259
Long live the Emperor!

28
00:01:12,859 --> 00:01:14,388
Quick history lesson.

29
00:01:14,588 --> 00:01:18,650
A few years earlier, France had a revolution and got rid of its king.

30
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:25,666
Now a young general from Corsica, Napoleon Bonaparte, was about to crown himself Emperor of the French.

31
00:01:25,866 --> 00:01:29,597
Even the Pope had travelled all the way from Rome for the ceremony.

32
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:33,575
Before I could escape, a nervous man grabbed my arm.

33
00:01:33,775 --> 00:01:36,442
Martin Biennais, the Emperor's goldsmith.

34
00:01:36,642 --> 00:01:39,371
Help me carry this to the cathedral, he said.

35
00:01:39,571 --> 00:01:44,358
It was a wreath of golden laurel leaves, like the ones Roman emperors wore.

36
00:01:44,958 --> 00:01:46,070
It was heavy.

37
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:47,513
Really heavy.

38
00:01:47,713 --> 00:01:50,732
So I did what any helpful time traveller would do.

39
00:01:50,932 --> 00:01:51,652
I tripped.

40
00:01:51,852 --> 00:01:55,321
Two golden leaves snapped off and rolled into my pocket.

41
00:01:55,521 --> 00:01:59,876
History says the wreath was so heavy that six leaves had to be removed.

42
00:02:00,076 --> 00:02:01,606
I only broke two.

43
00:02:01,806 --> 00:02:04,298
I'm not taking the blame for the other four.

44
00:02:04,898 --> 00:02:05,780
Fun fact.

45
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:10,666
One of those removed leaves was sold at auction in two thousand and seventeen.

46
00:02:10,865 --> 00:02:13,883
For six hundred and twenty five thousand euros.

47
00:02:14,083 --> 00:02:15,212
For one leaf.

48
00:02:15,812 --> 00:02:20,913
Then somebody shoved me onto the Emperor's golden carriage, pulled by eight horses.

49
00:02:21,113 --> 00:02:22,850
Nobody told me the way.

50
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:25,580
So I told the driver I knew a shortcut.

51
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:27,890
I did not know a shortcut.

52
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:29,167
We got lost.

53
00:02:29,367 --> 00:02:36,667
And inside Notre Dame, the Pope, who had travelled for weeks to get here, sat waiting in the freezing cathedral, for about an hour.

54
00:02:37,267 --> 00:02:40,391
When we finally arrived, Notre Dame looked amazing.

55
00:02:40,591 --> 00:02:41,859
Well, mostly.

56
00:02:42,059 --> 00:02:50,872
The Revolution had left the old cathedral badly damaged, so the organisers hid the cracks behind painted decorations, like a giant movie set.

57
00:02:51,472 --> 00:02:56,292
My next job: help carry the long velvet train of the new Empress, Josephine.

58
00:02:56,492 --> 00:02:59,399
The other train bearers were Napoleon's sisters.

59
00:02:59,599 --> 00:03:01,991
And they really did not like Josephine.

60
00:03:02,491 --> 00:03:04,819
Halfway down the aisle, they let go.

61
00:03:05,019 --> 00:03:06,067
All at once.

62
00:03:06,268 --> 00:03:08,833
The whole train landed on top of me.

63
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:13,479
I stepped on it, and Josephine got yanked backwards like a fish on a line.

64
00:03:14,079 --> 00:03:15,929
Then came the big moment.

65
00:03:16,129 --> 00:03:19,725
I was holding the crown on a velvet cushion, next to the altar.

66
00:03:19,925 --> 00:03:22,168
The Pope raised his hand to bless it.

67
00:03:22,368 --> 00:03:23,683
And I sneezed.

68
00:03:23,883 --> 00:03:26,372
A dusty, cathedral sized sneeze.

69
00:03:26,772 --> 00:03:28,732
The crown flew into the air.

70
00:03:28,932 --> 00:03:30,788
The whole cathedral gasped.

71
00:03:30,988 --> 00:03:36,335
And Napoleon, without even blinking, snatched it out of the air, and placed it on his own head.

72
00:03:36,935 --> 00:03:43,357
Historians say he planned to crown himself all along, to show that no one, not even the Pope, stood above him.

73
00:03:43,557 --> 00:03:44,749
And they're right.

74
00:03:44,949 --> 00:03:46,212
He did plan it.

75
00:03:46,412 --> 00:03:48,756
He just didn't plan to catch it first.

76
00:03:49,356 --> 00:03:51,512
Then he crowned Josephine himself.

77
00:03:51,712 --> 00:03:55,538
She knelt, and he placed a smaller crown on her head, very gently.

78
00:03:55,738 --> 00:03:58,396
The one moment of the day that went perfectly.

79
00:03:58,596 --> 00:04:01,657
Mostly because I was standing far, far away.

80
00:04:02,257 --> 00:04:04,649
Oh, and about that angry little man.

81
00:04:04,849 --> 00:04:07,132
Napoleon wasn't actually short.

82
00:04:07,332 --> 00:04:10,841
He was about one metre sixty nine, normal for his time.

83
00:04:11,041 --> 00:04:17,820
The myth came from French inches being longer than English ones, and from British cartoonists who loved drawing him tiny.

84
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:22,942
Watching all of it was the painter Jacques-Louis David, sketching like crazy.

85
00:04:23,142 --> 00:04:28,179
He spent the next three years on the painting, and added a few things that never happened.

86
00:04:28,379 --> 00:04:31,542
Like Napoleon's mother, sitting proudly in the middle.

87
00:04:31,742 --> 00:04:33,282
She wasn't even there.

88
00:04:33,482 --> 00:04:35,088
She had stayed in Rome.

89
00:04:35,688 --> 00:04:42,949
And for one terrible second, David looked up from his sketchbook, straight at the short guy in the red cap, who was still sneezing.

90
00:04:43,449 --> 00:04:45,732
Then the clocks started spinning again.

91
00:04:46,132 --> 00:04:47,760
Back at the exhibition.

92
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:49,660
Same giant painting.

93
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:51,211
I stepped closer.

94
00:04:51,411 --> 00:04:56,734
And there, in the crowd, behind a bishop, half hidden in the shadows, was a tiny red cap.

95
00:04:57,334 --> 00:05:00,888
Then I put my hand in my pocket, and felt something cold.

96
00:05:01,088 --> 00:05:02,186
And golden.

97
00:05:02,386 --> 00:05:03,396
Two leaves.

98
00:05:03,596 --> 00:05:06,247
At six hundred thousand euros each.

99
00:05:06,447 --> 00:05:08,493
I should probably give these back.

100
00:05:09,093 --> 00:05:10,001
So yes.

101
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:15,619
I made the Emperor late, tripped the Empress, and threw a crown at the most powerful man in Europe.

102
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:17,688
And he still became Emperor.

103
00:05:17,888 --> 00:05:18,852
Honestly?

104
00:05:19,052 --> 00:05:20,404
I think I helped.

105
00:05:21,204 --> 00:05:23,241
Next time, I'm staying home.

106
00:05:23,441 --> 00:05:25,021
I mean it this time.
